258-
259:### Admitting your own assertion entity
260-
261-A host whose model has its own assertion entity calls `admit()` with a
262-shape:
263-
264-```python
265-from gnd.examples.literature import assertion_admission as admission
266-
267-shape = {"format": "literature-assertion-shape/1", "assertion": "claim", "follow": ["quantity"]}
268-record = admission.admit(model, raw, policy, receipts, evidence, out, shape=shape)
269-```
270-
271-- **Identity.** An assertion is an instance of the named entity type,
272-  identified by its own handle. A type that objectifies a fact type is
273-  refused (`shape:objectified-assertion`), so the packaged `assertion` stays
274-  on the packaged shape above.
275-- **Attached facts.** A fact type is attached when one of its roles is
276-  played by the assertion type. Every attached row of an admitted assertion
277-  is kept, except a row that also names an assertion that was not admitted.
278-- **Followed facts.** For each instance of a type listed in `follow` that a
279-  kept row names, the rows of every fact type that type plays a role in are
280-  kept too, and the walk goes on from them. A type not listed is kept as a
281-  value only; with an empty list nothing is followed. A row naming an
282-  assertion that was not admitted is never kept, whichever way it is reached.
283-  The admitted population's `covers` lists the attached and followed fact
284-  types; other fact types are not copied.
285-- **Judged unit.** Policy targets are attached rows. An assertion is
286-  supported when at least one of its rows is a target and every target among
287-  them is admitted; a target on any other fact type is refused. The
288-  vocabulary and condition checks above apply to the packaged shape only:
289-  in a shaped call no value means "absent".
290-- **Bounds.** `max_rows` (default 16,384, at most 65,536) bounds the
291-  attached and followed rows read; it needs a shape.
292-- **Include roots.** A model that declares includes is admitted with its
293-  includes copied beside it; each include path must be relative.
294-- **Rules.** `rules` decides which supported assertions are admitted:
295-
296-  ```json
297:  {"format": "literature-admission-rules/1", "requirements": [
298-    {"id": "positive", "fact_type": "claim_polarity", "role": "polarity",
299-     "admissible": ["asserts"], "reasons": [{"value": "negates", "reason": "negated"}]}]}
300-  ```
301-
302-  An assertion satisfies a requirement when it has at least one row of the
303-  named fact type and every such row's role value is admissible; values are
304-  matched as JSON values, so `"1"` and `1` differ. Requirements are checked
305-  in order, and the first one that fails gives the reason: the one declared
306-  for the offending value, else the requirement's `id`. Under a shape a
307-  requirement names an attached fact type; on the packaged shape it names
308-  `assertion_statement`, and the packaged comparison is the default rules
309-  (`DEFAULT_RULES`: polarity first, then attribution). A requirement naming
310-  an unknown fact type or role is refused, never read as "no row matched".
311-- **Statuses.** In a `/2` record a supported assertion that fails a rule is
312-  `policy-refused` with that reason; an unsupported one is `fail`. A shaped
313:  call with rules adds an `admission-rules` stage, one row per assertion.
314-- **Target cap.** `max_targets` (1 to 1,024) refuses a policy with more
315-  targets; 1,024 is `gnd judgments check`'s own cap, so a larger set is
316-  admitted in several calls.
